From ea1d15954b5171092ba068cfa257c39d1f655578 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christopher A. Williamson" <contact@chrisaw.io>
Date: Fri, 3 Apr 2026 00:30:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] force HWC power-off during display init to fix MTK
 boot-black-screen
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

on MTK devices like the blackview shark 8 (MT6789), the bootloader
leaves the display powered on (HWC mode 2).  the kernel's
disable_unused clock framework then kills the DSI and display clocks
before userspace starts.  the hardware is off but the HWC's internal
state still says mode 2.

during initializeDisplays(), SurfaceFlinger tries to power-cycle
displays through OFF then ON.  however the OFF request is skipped
because SF's own internal state already reads OFF (its default).
the subsequent ON request reaches HWC, which sees current=2 and
requested=2, takes a shortcut 'already on' path that does a DRM
atomic commit (fails with -EINVAL because clocks are dead) and an
fb0 fallback (fails because /dev/fb0 doesn't exist).

fix by sending the OFF directly to HWC during the init OFF loop,
bypassing SF's redundant state check.  this resets HWC's internal
state from 2 to 0, so the following ON request triggers a proper
full 0→2 power-on sequence through the DRM/KMS path.
---
 services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
index eb481b1e1c..0bfb590b27 100644
--- a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
+++ b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
@@ -6060,8 +6060,18 @@ void SurfaceFlinger::initializeDisplays() {
         ftl::FakeGuard guard(mStateLock);
 
         // In case of a restart, ensure all displays are off.
+        // on some MTK devices the bootloader leaves the display on and the
+        // kernel's disable_unused clock framework kills the DSI clocks
+        // before userspace starts.  the HWC still thinks the display is
+        // powered (mode 2) while the hardware is actually off.  if we
+        // only call setPhysicalDisplayPowerMode(OFF) it gets skipped
+        // because SF's internal state already says OFF.  send the OFF
+        // directly to HWC so it resets its internal state, then the
+        // subsequent ON request does a proper full power-on sequence.
         for (const auto& [id, display] : mPhysicalDisplays) {
-            setPhysicalDisplayPowerMode(getDisplayDeviceLocked(id), hal::PowerMode::OFF);
+            const auto dev = getDisplayDeviceLocked(id);
+            getHwComposer().setPowerMode(id, hal::PowerMode::OFF);
+            dev->setPowerMode(hal::PowerMode::OFF);
         }
 
         // Power on all displays. The primary display is first, so becomes the active display. Also,
